Signs You Might Need One
A consultant isn't essential for every business right away. Consider one if:
Your marketing spend isn't delivering measurable returns
You're launching something new or entering a new market
Your team is executing tactics without a real strategy tying it together
Your previous approach has stopped working as you've grown
Leadership disagrees on direction and needs a neutral voice
You're about to hire an agency or build a team and want a plan first
What an Engagement Typically Looks Like
Discovery call. Learning about your business, goals, and frustrations.
Audit. Reviewing current marketing assets and performance data.
Market research. Understanding your standing versus competitors.
Strategy building. A documented roadmap with priorities and timelines.
Sharing findings. Presenting the plan to align stakeholders.
Implementation support. Some help with execution or team coaching.
Performance review. Measuring against goals and adjusting course.
Common Pitfalls to Avoid
Choosing on price alone. Cheap often means limited results.
Skipping discovery. Jumping to advice without understanding your business is a red flag.
Expecting hands-on execution. For daily campaign work, an agency or freelancer usually fits better.
No clear metrics. Without agreed goals, success is hard to measure.
Treating it as permanent. Strategy needs revisiting as things evolve.
What It Usually Costs
Rates vary based on experience, scope, and location. Consultants typically charge through:
Hourly rate, for quick or short-term needs
Project fee, for a clearly scoped deliverable
Monthly retainer, for ongoing support
More experienced, specialized consultants generally charge more than generalists. Asking for relevant case studies is always worthwhile.
Why Businesses Choose to Hire One
